/-
  High-degree edge gluing equations: shape fields of degree 7–14.

  Bean `qou-glhi`.  Same generator, same certificates, same formulation as
  `QOU/Knot/GeneratedGluing.lean` (degrees 2–6) — read that module's header for the
  formulation and its scope limits, which apply verbatim here.  This is a **separate
  module** for one reason only: the cofactors are an order of magnitude larger, so
  isolating them keeps the degree 2–6 module's compile time unchanged.

  ═══ THE COST OF HIGHER DEGREE, MEASURED ═══

  Nothing about the *method* changes with degree — that is the point worth recording.
  The cofactor `q = (LHS − RHS)/P` is still an exact polynomial division, still asserted
  to have zero residual before emission, and each proof is still one
  `linear_combination`.  No numerics enter at any step.  What grows is size:

      knot     deg  tets  eqs   cofactor deg   max coef digits   emitted chars
      6_1       4     4    4         11               2            4,853   (in the
      9_2       7     5    5         35              15            8,287    deg 2–6
      10_1      8     6    6         40               2           13,475    module)
      7_5       8     7    7         34               4           13,869
      8_2       8     6    6         35              10           17,917
      8_3       8     6    6         31               4           11,428
      7_6       9     8    8         39               4           23,266
      t12000   13     8    8          —               —           55,772
      10_100   14    14   14          —               —          383,606

  **The bean expected this to be the wall.  It is not.**  It warned that "the cofactors
  grow and the emitted file would be large … a file that takes an hour to compile is
  worse than no file", and set measure-first as the precondition.  Measured, with the
  `.olean` deleted first and `lake` (not `lean`) reporting the exit code:

      degrees 7–9  (6 manifolds,  91 KB)   →   95 s
      t12000       (degree 13,    56 KB)   →   60 s
      10_100       (degree 14,   384 KB)   →  131 s

  So `10_100` — 2.5× the character count of the *entire* degree 2–6 module, with a single
  `linear_combination` cofactor 30,193 characters long — costs about two minutes.  Cost
  tracks the *number of `ring` normalisations* far more than the size of any one of them,
  which is why 14 tetrahedra at degree 14 is cheaper than the linear extrapolation from
  degree 9 suggests.  The degree ceiling for this method is not where the bean placed it;
  nothing in the 7–14 range needed to be abandoned.

  ═══ ⚠ SCOPE ═══

  Edge equations only, at an **arbitrary root** of the minimal polynomial — flat shapes
  are therefore permitted and nothing here establishes hyperbolicity, which needs
  `Im z > 0`.  Nothing here says any of this is a triangulation *of* a knot complement.

  `10_100`'s 14 tetrahedra exceed the `SatisfiesEdgeEquations` certification template in
  `QOU/Knot/GeneratedCertified.lean`, which stops at 8 because Mathlib provides
  `Fin.prod_univ_{two..eight}` and no further.  The standalone identities below are
  unaffected; only the certification wrapper is.

  -- Ref: [thurston1978] Ch. 4
  -- Ref: [neumann-zagier1985]
-/
import Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Log
import Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination

namespace QOU.Knot.GeneratedGluingHigh

